<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flows" target="iran_proxy_escalation::maritime_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained increases in Western military aid to Ukraine prompt Russia and its partners to accelerate transfers of kamikaze drones, anti-ship missiles and exportable targeting gear to Iran and allied militias, which materially raise proxy capacity to execute maritime attacks.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_proxy_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flows" target="north_korea_missiles::arms_flow_volume" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.62</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained increases in Western military aid to Ukraine raise Russian incentives to offset losses and sanction-pressure by accelerating bilateral arms transfers and permissive brokering to partners like North Korea, raising the volume of weapons and delivery systems entering DPRK networks.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">north_korea_missiles</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::military_aid_flows" target="us_china_tech_trade::ai_capability_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.66</data>
  <data key="d37">Large, sustained military aid packages include procurement contracts, R&amp;D funding and classified data-sharing with defense contractors and national labs, which accelerates development and fielding of higher‑performance AI models relevant to both military and commercial deployments.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_trade</data>
</edge>
